WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.537
Step five matches the same
export with the example's rule.

2
00:00:03.537 --> 00:00:08.600
You load the export's rows and pass the
two emergency accounts' object I D's.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.450
Five rows come back, where
Microsoft Learn's query found two.

4
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.975
The two interactive sign-ins
are there, one for each account.

5
00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:17.150
And three more.

6
00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.834
A non-interactive sign-in
by the first account,

7
00:00:19.834 --> 00:00:23.437
which is what a refreshed token
in a stolen session looks like.

8
00:00:23.437 --> 00:00:27.876
An Add member to role event made by
the second account: a role change.

9
00:00:27.876 --> 00:00:31.945
And a User registered security
info event on the first account:

10
00:00:31.945 --> 00:00:36.563
a new method registered on an emergency
account, the start of a takeover.

11
00:00:36.563 --> 00:00:40.610
Those are the three events the
extra two tables exist to catch.
